---
import Doc from '../layouts/Doc.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const CORRESPONDENCES = [
  ['Ashi points, acupoints', 'China', 'Perforators, held', 'Heine: ~80% of points at fascial perforations; Langevin: 80% on connective-tissue planes; Maurer: bundles at only a few points'],
  ['Sinew channels and their knots (結)', 'China', 'Lines where the fascia is anchored to bone', 'Proposed; untested'],
  ['Cou li — the texture between skin and flesh', 'China', 'The superficial fascial plane', 'Proposed'],
  ['Propagated sensation along the channel', 'China', 'Conducted vasodilation along a perforator tree', 'Speeds overlap (cm/s); untested together'],
  ['Channel-knots (rtsa mdud) untied by the winds', 'Tibet', 'Held states released by breath', 'Proposed'],
  ['Granthis — Brahma, Viṣṇu, Rudra', 'India', 'Knots along the midline, the last between the brows (the front root of the galea)', 'Proposed'],
  ['Kuṇḍalinī rising; kriyās', 'India', 'Release travelling along trees; the fascia re-knitting', 'Documented as experience (Lindahl 2017); mechanism open'],
  ['Saṅkhāras as solidified sensations', 'Buddhist', 'Held states that dissolve under equanimous attention and breath', 'Proposed'],
  ['Rapture (pīti) suffusing the body', 'Buddhist', 'The skin’s small vessels opening widely at once — warmth, tingling, well-being', 'Proposed'],
  ['Cupping and gua sha', 'China', 'Tools for the fascia: lift and shear the superficial plane', 'Mechanically plausible'],
];
const index = [
  { id: 'reading', label: 'reading' },
  { id: 'china', label: 'China' },
  { id: 'daoist', label: 'Daoist' },
  { id: 'india', label: 'Yoga · tantra' },
  { id: 'tibet', label: 'Tibet' },
  { id: 'buddhist', label: 'Buddhist' },
  { id: 'japan', label: 'Japan' },
  { id: 'table', label: 'correspondences' },
];
---
<Doc
  title="Maps of the inner body"
  kicker="Traditions"
  current="traditions"
  lede="Nearly every tradition that mapped the inner body arrived at the same three claims: there are knots, breath is the tool, and untying them changes the mind."
  description="Maps of the inner body from Chinese medicine, Daoist alchemy, yoga and tantra, Tibetan tsa lung and Dzogchen, and Buddhist practice — read beside the anatomy of perforators and fascia."
  glyph="経"
  index={index}
>
  <section id="reading" class="sec" data-glyph="読">
    <h2>A note on reading</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        These maps were drawn from the inside, in their own vocabularies, over two thousand years. They are not anatomy, and nothing here
        claims they were secretly describing it. The claim is narrower: where the traditions and modern reports agree on a <em>place</em> in
        the body, there is often a real structure there — a correspondence specific enough to be checked blind. Correspondence found after
        the fact is the weakest kind of evidence. In time each map will be drawn on the same body in the{' '}
        <a href={`${base}/atlas/`}>atlas</a>, so the maps can be compared point by point.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="china" class="sec" data-glyph="経絡">
    <h2>Points, channels, and the knots of the sinews</h2>
    <p class="label">China · Huangdi Neijing and after</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Chinese medicine works with two kinds of structure. The first are points. The tender <em>ashi</em> points, named by Sun Simiao for
        the patient’s cry when they are pressed, follow one rule: take the painful spot as the point. The 361 classical acupoints lie along
        twelve primary channels and two midline vessels — the Governor (Du) up the back and the Conception (Ren) down the front.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is the fascia. The <em>Neijing</em> describes the <em>cou li</em>, the interstices between skin and flesh, through which
        the defensive qi circulates and pathogenic “wind” enters. It also describes the <em>jing jin</em>, twelve sinew channels of muscle and
        connective tissue, each of which <em>knots</em> — 結, jié, to bind or tie — at bony prominences such as the ankle, knee, hip, shoulder
        and cheekbone, and whose treatment is heat at the painful spot. Needling and acupressure act at points; cupping and gua sha act on
        the plane, lifting and shearing it until “sha” rises.
      </p>
      <p>
        For decades, Chinese researchers have also documented <em>propagated sensation along the channel</em>: stimulating a point produces
        warmth or tingling that travels along the meridian at one to ten centimetres a second and is halted by pressure or cold — much the
        profile of a dilation travelling along a tree of vessels.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="daoist" class="sec" data-glyph="周天">
    <h2>The orbit</h2>
    <p class="label">Daoist internal alchemy</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        In <em>neidan</em>, internal alchemy, the practitioner circulates breath-energy around the <em>microcosmic orbit</em>: up the
        Governor vessel along the spine and over the head, then down the Conception vessel in front, through gates where the flow is said to
        be blocked until the work opens them. Three dantian — below the navel, at the heart and between the brows — are the fields in which
        the work is done. The manuals counsel patience: the orbit opens over years, and is not to be forced.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="india" class="sec" data-glyph="輪">
    <h2>Nāḍīs, cakras, and the three knots</h2>
    <p class="label">Yoga and tantra</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The yogic body is a network of <em>nāḍīs</em> — seventy-two thousand in the Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā, three hundred and fifty thousand in
        the Śiva Saṃhitā — of which three matter most: <em>suṣumṇā</em> in the centre, with <em>iḍā</em> and <em>piṅgalā</em> winding on
        either side. Along the central channel sit the <em>cakras</em>, and at three of them, knots: the <strong>granthis</strong> of Brahmā
        low in the body, of Viṣṇu at the heart, and of Rudra between the brows. Breath and attention pierce them in turn, and each piercing
        marks a stage.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the kuṇḍalinī literature the energy is a serpent coiled at the base of the spine, rising through the knots to the crown. The
        rising is described as heat, currents and involuntary movement — the <em>kriyās</em> — and sometimes as a crisis that takes years to
        settle. One number is worth holding lightly: seventy-two thousand channels, and about a hundred thousand small perforators.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tibet" class="sec" data-glyph="脈風">
    <h2>Channels, winds, drops — and knots</h2>
    <p class="label">Tibet · tsa lung and Dzogchen</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Tibetan practice works with <em>tsa</em>, channels; <em>lung</em>, winds; and <em>tigle</em>, drops. A central channel runs from the
        crown to below the navel, with right and left channels beside it that coil around it at the channel-wheels, forming{' '}
        <strong>channel-knots</strong> (<em>rtsa mdud</em>), tightest at the heart. The Six Yogas and the tsa lung exercises untie them by
        working the winds with the breath — and the traditions warn against untying them too fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Dzogchen adds a channel of its own, the <em>kati</em>, a crystal tube joining the heart to the eyes, and makes the tradition’s most
        extreme claim about the body: the <strong>rainbow body</strong> (<em>’ja’ lus</em>), in which a realised practitioner’s body is said to
        shrink at death and dissolve into light, leaving only hair and nails. On the perforator view the endpoint is humbler, and measurable:
        a body with all its perforators open and none of its knots.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="buddhist" class="sec" data-glyph="龍">
    <h2>Saṅkhāras, and the serpent over the head</h2>
    <p class="label">Buddhist practice and iconography</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        In the Vipassana taught by S. N. Goenka, gross, solid bodily sensations are <em>saṅkhāras</em> — congealed reactions — that dissolve
        under equanimous attention. This is almost word for word what happens on a foam roller, with attention as the probe. The suttas
        describe the rapture of deep concentration suffusing the whole body, as water kneaded into a ball of bath powder soaks it through,
        within and without — the well-being that can come with a release.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the awakening, the Udāna says, a great storm came, and the nāga king <strong>Mucalinda</strong> coiled around the seated Buddha
        and spread his hood over his head for seven days. In the iconography the hood has seven heads and forms a canopy over the crown,
        where the <em>uṣṇīṣa</em> rises from the top of the Buddha’s head. It is imagery, not evidence — but serpents appear wherever the
        traditions describe what moves in the body: kuṇḍalinī coiled at the base of the spine, the nāga’s hood over the crown, and the
        snake-like rising some practitioners describe today.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="japan" class="sec" data-glyph="凝り">
    <h2>Kori</h2>
    <p class="label">Japan</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Japanese has an everyday word for a knot: <em>kori</em> (凝り), a congealing — and <em>katakori</em>, stiff shoulders, is among the most
        common complaints in the country. Shiatsu presses <em>tsubo</em>, points; Zen training gathers the breath into the <em>hara</em>,
        below the navel.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="table" class="sec full" data-glyph="対照">
    <h2>Proposed correspondences</h2>
    <p class="label">Read beside the anatomy</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Traditional structure</th><th>Tradition</th><th>Proposed correlate</th><th>Status</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {CORRESPONDENCES.map((r) => (
            <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="fine prose">
      Each could be checked blind. Are the classical points perforators? Are the knots of the sinew channels the lines where the fascia is
      anchored? Are the channels the trees and planes between them?
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>

